Cloud Operations Engineer at Pega Systems

Cloud Operations Engineer (1–3) role for the Pega Operations Center team in Bangalore.

The process included HR screening → Technical round → Technical + Managerial panel round.

.
🧩 Round 1 - Technical (~1 hr)

Covered Linux → AWS → K8s, starting with basics and moving into intermediate topics.

Since I was fresh from my CKA prep, K8s topics went very well; AWS storage topics were slightly weaker.

The interviewer was supportive throughout and even guided me through a few answers, making a positive interview experience.

Some of the questions I recall:

1. Linux
- Tail last few lines of a file
- Disable password auth in SSH
- Troubleshoot SSH auth via Session Manager
- Fix EC2 access after bad SSH config
- Symlink vs Hardlink

2. AWS
- S3 getObject permission denied - troubleshoot?
- EC2 instance not showing in console
- Attach & mount new EBS volume without downtime
- What CloudFormation is used for
- Terraform - launch an EC2 inside an existing VPC? Explained the steps

3. Kubernetes
- Internal architecture of K8s
- Explain CoreDNS & service discovery
- Role of ETCD
- HPA
- Pod stuck in Pending - troubleshooting steps
- Service types & Ingress in K8s
- Deployment vs StatefulSet vs DaemonSet
- Deployment strategies for zero downtime
- ServiceAccounts
- Troubleshooting intermittently crashing pod

It went smoothly and got positive feedback at the end and was moved to the next round.

🧩 Round 2 - Technical + Managerial (<1 hr)

Started with a technical discussion about my self-hosted K8s project - questions like - why self-hosted over managed cluster, about networking and how the app is exposed, etc.

Also covered some scenario question around CoreDNS setup in my cluster (cannot recall the exact question), which led to some more basic DNS questions (record types & DNS resolution)

Finished the tech discussion with some K8s questions, most of them were repeated from the first round, so this part went smoothly.

Followed by the managerial discussion - covering my experience, career gap (no issues there 🤷‍♂️), academics, etc.

The only concern was my ongoing online MCA program. They wanted clarity on how I would balance the weekend MCA classes with a rotational on-call schedule including weekends. While the program is flexible, I couldn’t articulate a concrete plan in that moment (I fumbled 😅).

Got positive feedback, yet a few days later, I received the rejection email (no reason, but I assume it was the MCA).

I tried to reach out to the manager to better explain, but that didn't work 😅

💡Takeaway: Overall, the interview experience was good. However, final decisions often come down to factors like cultural fit, role constraints, and commitment expectations etc.

👉 And always be prepared with answers to backup your situation, being transparent in interviews does not mean being unprepared.
